#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152029 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152219 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a8bf202-99c5-5bfd-8df3-5bd27cd65640" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Polynomial Vector Space Given Its Standard Monomial Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "2bcc7bb9-1b5e-5858-811d-1e5c14f0bc29" :question/math-academy-id "e-8288" :question/problem "What is the dimension of $\\mathbb{R}_6[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $6?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$ is\n\n$$\n\\{1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, \\: t^n\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_6[t]$ is \n\n$$\n\\{1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, \\: t^6\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_6[t])=6+1=7.\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_n[t])=n+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152030 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aee17e3a-031d-5c0e-9488-17410b3a2372" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Dimensions of Vector Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "e1ae146f-04ae-5662-9ea3-178a23b3c366" :question/math-academy-id "e-8289" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $9?$\n\nI. $\\mathbb{R}^8$\n\nII. $\\mathbb{R}_8[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $8$\n\nIII. $\\text{M}_{3}(\\mathbb{R}),$ the vector space of all $3 \\times 3$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\nI. Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n)=n.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^8)=8\\neq 9.\\quad {\\color{red}{\\times}}\n  $$\n\nII. Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_{8}[t])=8+1=9.\\quad {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n  $$\n\nIII. Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{n}(\\mathbb{R}))=n^2.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}(\\text{M}_{3}(\\mathbb{R}))=3^2=9.\\quad {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n  $$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152031 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e609aabf-7e7e-5997-b8dc-c66e7a14a36f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "6d2ba662-f58e-56ec-bf87-c1f26c12c1c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8290" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\nI. $\\mathbb{R}^{100}$\n\nII. $\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R}),$ the vector space of all $50 \\times 100$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$\n\nIII. $\\mathbb{R}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomial functions in the variable $t$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}^{100}$ and $\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R})$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^{100}) = 100\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R})) = 50\\cdot 100 = 5\\,000\n$$\n\n \nare both finite numbers.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of (polynomial) functions is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n\\big\\{ 1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, t^n \\big\\},\\quad n\\in \\mathbb N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n\\sum\\limits_{i=0}^n a_i t^i = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $t \\in \\mathbb{R}$ if and only if $a_i = 0$ for all $i=0,1,2,\\ldots, n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in \\mathbb N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ of size $n,$ which imples that $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152032 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152222 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b61a755f-23e8-5039-b2f5-b5984fdb420c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Matrix Vector Space Given Its Standard Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "61fa85c6-5b27-5f81-a7ba-363eb68e8d3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8315" :question/problem "What is the dimension of $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R}),$ the vector space of all $5\\times 6$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of $\\text{M}_{n\\times m}(\\mathbb{R})$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of $\\text{M}_{3\\times 2} (\\mathbb{R})$ contains $3\\cdot 2= 6$ elements, and is given by\n\n![](images/8a/8a50f2d0ea7a7d24ca498b7e326bd66fb9afcfc089159146f07018c96fecfaf8.svg)\n\nHowever, we're asked to find the dimension of $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R}),$ not $\\text{M}_{3\\times 2} (\\mathbb{R}).$\n\nIf we were to repeat the same process above for the vector space $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R}),$ we would have $30$ matrices in our basis, meaning that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{5\\times 6}(\\mathbb{R})) = 5\\cdot 6 =30.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{n\\times m}(\\mathbb{R})) = n\\cdot m.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152219 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a8bf202-99c5-5bfd-8df3-5bd27cd65640" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Polynomial Vector Space Given Its Standard Monomial Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13e3157e-c1fe-5d1b-ad52-732d9e91a6df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241349} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3b14ba-f7ee-53b8-bb9e-930a6892e7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242020} {:answer/id #uuid "a295a99f-a442-5b1c-850a-902695aec1a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242602} {:answer/id #uuid "c10a882c-6981-5092-9e0e-0e344cd07b36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242871} {:answer/id #uuid "f76477fb-7405-5db0-bca5-b7944f200234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13e3157e-c1fe-5d1b-ad52-732d9e91a6df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241349} :answer-field/id #uuid "223ceb91-14ae-5a21-a208-e1e86c099cdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d133675-64d5-50d6-9a87-de7265046742" :question/math-academy-id "q-106868" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${R}_{24}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $24?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${R}_{n}[t]$ is\n\n$$\n\\{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{n}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, the standard basis of ${R}_{24}[t]$ is \n\n$$\n\\{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{24}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{24}[t])=24+1=25.\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397877 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152222 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b61a755f-23e8-5039-b2f5-b5984fdb420c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Matrix Vector Space Given Its Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22b9d3a8-da80-5055-a4ac-61a8de4abec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397879} {:answer/id #uuid "6b5db82b-df8d-5504-91e7-fcc98356c581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397880} {:answer/id #uuid "9af72902-6c61-5539-95d7-3ad5251165aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397881} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb6755f-48c1-5f6b-9cd2-eb9cbe4c57c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397882} {:answer/id #uuid "6ba279e7-9cb7-5403-9454-8ad5b15d2a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22b9d3a8-da80-5055-a4ac-61a8de4abec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397879} :answer-field/id #uuid "94ee78a5-d74a-542f-9cb8-9d156de061c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90418efb-5f81-5124-80f6-ce7467b7ec0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-106888" :question/problem "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{8}(R),$ the vector space of all $8\\times 8$ matrices over $R?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n}(R)$ contains a total of ${n}^{2}$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ contains ${2}^{2}=4$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{8}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{8}(R))={8}^{2}=64.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(R))={n}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484271 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aee17e3a-031d-5c0e-9488-17410b3a2372" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Dimensions of Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "009a8a79-709d-5587-ad2d-d2fc9b774237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483570} {:answer/id #uuid "01f93fad-b91d-5d83-8760-997fde54eb2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483572} {:answer/id #uuid "3afef46f-fb68-5e1e-b10a-4ddb1eb3b89e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483677} {:answer/id #uuid "56ca0553-38e4-50b8-9a28-89a7241ec7f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483724} {:answer/id #uuid "973cca31-9547-529d-9e9d-26a46076e6cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01f93fad-b91d-5d83-8760-997fde54eb2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483572} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cc0ee2b-b921-5134-908e-7834e4e7600e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2dac37f-1586-5572-8aef-afd7b8fea459" :question/math-academy-id "q-106898" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $15?$\n\nI. ${R}^{14}$\n\nII. ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 5}(R),$ the vector space of all $3\\times 5$ matrices over $R$\n\nIII. ${R}_{14}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $14$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\nI. Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}({R}^{14})=14\\ne 15.\\,\\times \n  $$\n\nII. Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{3\\times 5}(R))=3\\cdot 5=15.\\,✓\n  $$\n\nIII. Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}({R}_{14}[t])=14+1=15.\\,✓\n  $$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aee17e3a-031d-5c0e-9488-17410b3a2372" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Dimensions of Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32552e06-20c1-57b7-9224-0ed5f4066d64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483655} {:answer/id #uuid "590d536d-8a57-521b-a89d-2b171086d76f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483732} {:answer/id #uuid "89d8c30d-ba4a-5e14-b188-80139ba64458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483852} {:answer/id #uuid "bd0d3f41-d1ae-5d58-8cb3-4155120ea018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483941} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf69641-b623-5dba-9cfc-55e1971dc4c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32552e06-20c1-57b7-9224-0ed5f4066d64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483655} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c20ccce-26a4-5559-bfff-e726611193da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa7720d1-24b7-56bb-ae25-fc2830d10498" :question/math-academy-id "q-106899" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $7?$\n\nI. ${\\text{M}}_{7}(R),$ the vector space of all $7\\times 7$ matrices over $R$\n\nII. ${R}^{7}$\n\nIII. ${R}_{7}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $7$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\nI. Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(R))={n}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{7}(R))={7}^{2}=49\\ne 7.\\,\\times \n  $$\n\nII. Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}({R}^{7})=7.\\,✓\n  $$\n\nIII. Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}({R}_{7}[t])=7+1=8.\\,\\times \n  $$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aee17e3a-031d-5c0e-9488-17410b3a2372" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Dimensions of Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85f50e01-4432-5abc-a190-7e72cdc3873a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461479} {:answer/id #uuid "26b77efa-1ca5-50e3-a239-